<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ösung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Namde Ösung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ösung was a son of the Tibetan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Langdarma</a> whose claim to the throne, disputed by his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yumten">Yumten</a> after Langdarma's death around 842, divided the court and contributed to the collapse of the Tibetan Empire. Through his son Pelkhortsen and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyide-nyimagon">Kyide Nyimagon</a>, his line went on to found the dynasties of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, including the kingdom of Guge.</p></div>
